=== L5 Selecting Materials for Manufacture ===
id: ef51e6fe-ef81-4917-b8a3-109a4be0e7f0
slug: lesson-05
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:56:04.602307+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How engineers match material properties, cost, availability, aesthetics and sustainability to product requirements.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Selection Problem</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Choosing the right material means balancing five competing factors: functional requirements (the product must work), cost (affordable), availability (obtainable in the right form), aesthetics (appropriate appearance), and sustainability (environmentally responsible). The specification covers all five, and exam questions regularly ask students to justify a material choice against two or more of them.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Properties vs Requirements</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The primary criterion is always whether the material's properties meet the product's functional requirements. A <dfn class="term" data-def="The set of performance demands — mechanical, physical, thermal, or chemical — that a material must satisfy for a component to function correctly in service.">functional requirement</dfn> is a specific performance demand: the bracket must support 500 N without yielding; the casing must resist impact. Engineers map requirements onto properties: a spring requires elasticity; a cutting tool requires hardness; a body panel requires malleability. A material is eliminated if any critical property falls short, regardless of its other advantages.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n5">Engineers may use property charts (such as Ashby charts) to shortlist candidates — a shaft that must be stiff and lightweight might shortlist carbon fibre, titanium, and aluminium alloy before cost and availability narrow the choice. All the properties covered earlier — strength, elasticity, ductility, hardness, malleability, machinability — feed into this stage.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="For any material selection question, write your answer in this order: (1) functional requirement, (2) property that meets it, (3) material that has that property, (4) any trade-off with another criterion. Practise this structure on three past-paper scenarios.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Material selection always starts with functional requirements — a material that does not meet the minimum performance demands cannot be chosen, regardless of cost or sustainability advantages. Properties are the gating criterion.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Cost</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Cost has two specification components: <strong>material cost</strong> (price per kilogram or standard section) and <strong>manufacturing cost</strong> (tooling, energy, labour and machine time to process it). They can pull in opposite directions: CRP has a very high material cost but its low density means less material per component, yet manufacturing requires autoclaves, adding significant processing cost. Aluminium has moderate material cost but high machinability, giving low manufacturing cost for CNC-milled parts. A <dfn class="term" data-def="The total expenditure incurred at every stage of manufacture — raw material, processing, assembly, finishing and overheads — attributed to a single component.">unit cost</dfn> analysis considers both together to give the true cost per component.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How scale of production affects material cost decisions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">For a one-off prototype, a higher material cost per kilogram may be acceptable if the material is easy to hand-process, minimising manufacturing cost. For mass production of 100,000 units, even a small reduction in material cost per kilogram saves a large total sum, justifying precision tooling investment. The tooling cost for injection moulding or die casting is only recovered at high volume — for one-offs, machining a polymer block (more material waste, but no tooling) is usually cheaper.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Availability</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Even the ideal material on paper is useless if it cannot be obtained when needed. <dfn class="term" data-def="The extent to which a material can be sourced in the required form, quantity and lead time to support a given manufacturing programme.">Availability</dfn> covers three aspects: stock form (is the material supplied as bar, sheet, tube, or extrusion in the needed profile?), lead time (how quickly can it be delivered?), and supply certainty (single-supplier risk?). Low carbon steel, aluminium alloy, ABS, and acrylic are widely stocked and delivered within days. Specialist materials such as Nitinol SMA wire or engineering ceramics can have lead times of weeks from only a few global suppliers, making supply certainty a key concern at production scale.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Aesthetics</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Aesthetics — the visual and tactile qualities of a material — matter when the material is visible in the finished product. Colour, texture, translucency and achievable surface finish are all aesthetic properties. PMMA/acrylic is chosen for display cases for its optical clarity and mirror-polish capability. Stainless steel conveys quality and hygiene in kitchen and medical products. Brass is chosen for fittings and instruments partly for its warm golden colour. Between two functionally equivalent materials, aesthetics can be the deciding factor, especially for consumer products where appearance drives purchasing.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="List the five selection criteria: properties vs requirements, material cost, manufacturing cost, availability, aesthetics, sustainability. For each, write one sentence explaining why it matters and one scenario where it could be the deciding factor.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five material selection criteria are: properties versus requirements, cost (material and manufacturing), availability, aesthetics, and sustainability. In most engineering scenarios more than one criterion is used simultaneously to shortlist and then select the final material.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Sustainability</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><dfn class="term" data-def="The consideration of environmental impact across the full lifecycle of a material — extraction, processing, use, and disposal or recycling — when making engineering decisions.">Sustainability</dfn> considers the full material lifecycle: extraction (mining energy and habitat disruption), processing (smelting or polymerisation emissions), product life (durability, repairability), and end-of-life (recycling potential, biodegradability). Key examples: primary aluminium has very high embodied energy, but recycled aluminium uses only ~5% of that — closed-loop recycling is essential. PLA is made from renewable plant starch and is industrially compostable, unlike petroleum-derived ABS. Steel has the highest global recycling rate of any engineering material and retains properties through multiple cycles. Sustainability requirements are now embedded in procurement policies and environmental legislation.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Balancing the Criteria</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Real decisions balance all five criteria. A product might use a slightly less strong material if it is cheaper and more available. A consumer product might pay for a better-looking material if aesthetics justify a higher price. Sustainability is now weighted more heavily as companies must meet environmental procurement standards. The exam tests this balancing directly: 'evaluate the selection of a material' questions require at least two criteria and a reasoned judgement, not a list of properties.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Structuring a material selection evaluation answer</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Use this four-step structure for evaluation answers. (1) State the functional requirement: 'the housing must resist impact outdoors.' (2) Name the property and a material that meets it: 'polycarbonate has very high impact strength and can be UV-stabilised.' (3) Evaluate cost: 'polycarbonate is more expensive than HIPS but its impact resistance avoids product recalls.' (4) Consider sustainability: 'polycarbonate is not biodegradable, but its long service life lowers replacement frequency and overall environmental impact.' End with a clear justified conclusion. This structure addresses multiple criteria and reaches a recommendation — both needed for Mastering marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Material selection balances five criteria: properties versus functional requirements, cost (material and manufacturing), availability, aesthetics, and sustainability — all five may be relevant in a single engineering decision.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Properties are the gating criterion — a material that fails to meet the minimum performance requirements is eliminated regardless of how well it performs on other criteria.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Exam evaluation answers must name specific materials, link properties to requirements, acknowledge at least one trade-off, and reach a justified conclusion to achieve Mastering marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">Material selection questions carry the most marks because they require genuine reasoning, not just recall. When a question says 'justify your material choice', you must link properties directly to the product requirements stated in the scenario — do not give a generic list. Always acknowledge a trade-off: for example, 'aluminium alloy is preferred for its machinability and corrosion resistance, although its lower tensile strength compared with steel means a larger cross-section is required.' A balanced evaluation that reaches a clear conclusion will score Mastering; a list of correct points without a conclusion will score Secure at best.</p>

