=== L8 Health & Safety in Engineering Manufacture ===
id: 20700154-1abb-4c0c-b5a2-8476bb010dfa
slug: lesson-08
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:24:52.133403+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 20

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Risk assessment, PPE, workshop hazards and the control measures engineers use to keep people safe.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Health and Safety Matters in Engineering</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Engineering workshops and manufacturing facilities contain some of the most hazardous environments in any industry. Rotating machinery, sharp tools, hot materials, heavy loads, electrical equipment, and airborne particles all pose real risks to operators. Legal obligations under the Health and Safety at Work Act 1974 place a duty on employers to protect workers, and on employees to cooperate with safety procedures. Beyond legal compliance, good <dfn class="term" data-def="The policies, procedures and practices put in place to protect workers from injury, ill health and harm in the workplace.">health and safety</dfn> practice reduces accidents, prevents costly downtime, protects equipment, and enables engineers to focus on productive work rather than incident management.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Risk Assessment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A systematic process of identifying hazards in a workplace activity, evaluating the likelihood and severity of harm, and specifying control measures to reduce risk to an acceptable level.">risk assessment</dfn> is the cornerstone of engineering safety management. It follows a defined process: identify the hazard (anything with the potential to cause harm), decide who might be harmed and how, evaluate the risk (likelihood × severity), implement control measures to reduce the risk, and review the assessment regularly or when conditions change. In an engineering context, hazards might include swarf (metal chips) from turning operations, solvent fumes during finishing, or a guillotine blade during shearing. For each hazard, the engineer records the risk level and the specific control measures in place.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Risk is assessed on two dimensions: the <em>likelihood</em> of the hazard causing harm, and the <em>severity</em> of that harm if it occurs. A high-likelihood, high-severity risk (for example, unguarded rotating shafts) demands immediate and robust control. A low-likelihood, low-severity risk (for example, tripping on a cable on a well-managed shop floor) requires attention but is lower priority. The aim is never to eliminate all risk — that is rarely possible — but to reduce it to an acceptable and reasonably practicable level.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Learn the five steps of risk assessment: identify the hazard, decide who is harmed and how, evaluate the risk, implement control measures, review and update. You may be asked to complete or critique a risk assessment in the exam.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five steps of risk assessment are: (1) identify the hazard, (2) decide who might be harmed and how, (3) evaluate the risk (likelihood × severity), (4) implement control measures, (5) review and update the assessment. In engineering, this process is applied to every operation involving machinery, tools or hazardous materials.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Common Hazards in Engineering Manufacture</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Different manufacturing operations present different hazards. During <em>machining</em> (turning, milling, drilling), swarf is a major hazard — sharp metal chips can cut skin, enter eyes, or jam machinery; the rotating chuck or workpiece can catch loose clothing. During <em>welding and brazing</em>, UV radiation from the arc can cause permanent eye damage, fumes are toxic, and the workpiece and surrounding area reach extreme temperatures. During <em>casting</em>, molten metal is typically above 700 °C for aluminium and much hotter for ferrous metals, making burns a severe risk; steam can erupt if moisture is present in the mould. During <em>finishing processes</em> such as spray painting and powder coating, solvent fumes and fine particles are respiratory and fire hazards.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Noise-induced hearing loss is a long-term hazard from sustained exposure to machine noise above 85 dB(A). Manual handling of heavy workpieces risks musculoskeletal injury. Electrical hazards arise from power tools and machine supplies. A thorough risk assessment must account for all hazards relevant to the specific operation being carried out, not just the obvious ones.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Hierarchy of control measures — which come first?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Control measures are prioritised from most to least effective. Elimination (remove the hazard entirely) is best but often impractical in manufacturing. Substitution (replace a hazardous material or process with a safer one) is next. Engineering controls (machine guards, local exhaust ventilation, interlocks) reduce exposure without relying on human behaviour. Administrative controls (safe operating procedures, training, supervision, permit-to-work systems) rely on people following rules. PPE (personal protective equipment) is the last line of defence — it does not eliminate the hazard but protects the wearer if all other controls fail. PPE should never be the only control measure for a significant hazard.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Personal Protective Equipment (PPE)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Equipment worn by an individual to protect against health and safety risks in the workplace; the last line of defence when other control measures cannot fully eliminate a hazard.">Personal protective equipment (PPE)</dfn> is selected to match the specific hazards of each operation. Eye protection (safety spectacles or a full face shield) is mandatory for all machining, grinding, welding and casting operations — swarf and sparks travel at high velocity and metal splashes are unpredictable. Hearing protection (ear defenders or earplugs) is required where noise levels exceed 85 dB(A), including near grinding machines, pneumatic tools and loud presses. Welding operations require a welding helmet or auto-darkening shield to protect against UV radiation, in addition to fire-resistant gloves and a leather apron to guard against burns and spatter. Respiratory protection (dust mask or respirator) is needed when sanding, grinding composite materials (which produce carcinogenic fibres), spray painting or powder coating.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Footwear with steel toecaps protects against dropped heavy components. Gloves protect against cuts from sharp edges — but must never be worn near rotating machinery such as a lathe or drill press, because they can be snagged, pulling the hand into the machine. This is a counter-intuitive but important safety rule that examiners frequently test.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Remember: gloves must NOT be worn near rotating machinery — they can be caught and pull the operator's hand in. This is a high-frequency exam trap. Learn which PPE matches which hazard.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>PPE must be matched to the hazard: eye protection for machining/welding/casting; hearing protection above 85 dB(A); welding helmet + fire-resistant gloves + leather apron for welding; respiratory protection for composite grinding, spray painting and powder coating. Gloves must NOT be worn near rotating machinery as they risk entanglement.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: thinking PPE is always the best control measure</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Many students list PPE as the primary or only control measure when answering risk assessment questions. This will lose marks. PPE sits at the bottom of the control hierarchy — it is the last resort when engineering controls and administrative controls cannot fully eliminate the hazard. For maximum marks, describe the engineering control first (machine guard, extraction system, interlock), the administrative control second (training, SOP, supervision), and then PPE as the supplementary protection layer.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">A risk assessment identifies hazards, evaluates likelihood and severity, and specifies control measures — following five steps — and must be reviewed regularly or when conditions change.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Control measures follow a hierarchy: elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, then PPE — PPE is the last resort, not the first response.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">PPE must be matched to the specific hazard: eye protection for machining and welding, hearing protection above 85 dB(A), welding helmet and leather apron for welding, respiratory protection for grinding composites or spray painting — and gloves must never be worn near rotating machinery.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">Risk assessment questions often ask you to complete a partially filled table: hazard, who is at risk, control measure. Always state a specific hazard (not just 'machinery') and a specific control measure (not just 'be careful'). For PPE questions, match the PPE precisely to the hazard — if the question is about drilling, the answer is safety spectacles to protect against swarf, not a hard hat. Remember that gloves must not be worn near rotating machinery — this is frequently tested. If asked to evaluate a risk assessment, check whether it covers all significant hazards, whether the control measures are adequate, and whether it has been reviewed recently.</p>

