=== L9 Inventory Management & Lean Manufacturing ===
id: bc048811-535f-4ced-af4f-411d7cffe680
slug: lesson-09
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:48:12.427084+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 18

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Just-in-time, material requirements planning, the seven wastes of lean, and how reducing waste improves production.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Managing What You Need, When You Need It</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every manufacturing business must manage its inventory — the raw materials, components and finished goods it holds in stock. Too much stock ties up capital, occupies warehouse space, risks obsolescence, and can mask inefficiencies in the production process. Too little stock means production lines stop because a critical component has run out. <dfn class="term" data-def="The management of the ordering, storage and movement of materials and components to ensure production needs are met without holding excessive stock.">Inventory management</dfn> is the discipline of finding and maintaining the right balance, using systems such as just-in-time (JIT) and material requirements planning (MRP) to coordinate supply with demand.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Just-in-Time (JIT) Manufacturing</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="A production philosophy in which materials and components are ordered and delivered only when they are needed in the production process, eliminating the need for large stock holdings.">Just-in-time (JIT) manufacturing</dfn> is an approach in which materials arrive exactly when they are needed in the production sequence — not before and not after. Originated and perfected by Toyota in Japan, JIT aims to eliminate the waste of holding inventory. Advantages include: reduced warehousing costs, lower capital tied up in stock, reduced risk of materials becoming obsolete or damaged in storage, and the ability to respond quickly to changes in customer demand. Disadvantages include: very high dependency on supplier reliability — if a delivery is late, the production line stops; there is no buffer stock to absorb disruptions; and the approach requires excellent supplier relationships, accurate demand forecasting and a very disciplined production schedule. JIT works best in stable, predictable production environments.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="For a JIT exam question, always give at least one advantage AND one disadvantage. The most common disadvantage is that a late supplier delivery stops the entire production line — make this specific.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Just-in-time (JIT) manufacturing orders and delivers materials only when needed, eliminating large stock holdings. Key advantage: reduced warehousing cost and capital tied up in stock. Key disadvantage: total dependency on supplier reliability — a late delivery stops production immediately with no buffer stock to absorb the delay.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Material Requirements Planning (MRP)</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="A computer-based system that calculates what materials are needed, in what quantity and by when, based on the production schedule and the bill of materials for each product.">Material requirements planning (MRP)</dfn> is a computer-based approach that takes the production schedule (what products are to be made, in what quantities and by when) and works backwards through the bill of materials for each product to calculate exactly what raw materials and components must be ordered and when. MRP provides detailed ordering schedules, reduces the risk of running out of stock, and highlights when resources such as machine capacity and labour are insufficient to meet the schedule. Advantages include improved coordination between production and procurement, reduced waste from over-ordering, and better visibility of future capacity requirements. Disadvantages include the significant cost and complexity of implementing and maintaining MRP software, and the fact that MRP is only as accurate as the data fed into it — errors in the bill of materials or inaccurate lead-time data will cascade into incorrect ordering decisions.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does MRP differ from JIT?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">JIT is a production philosophy — it drives the entire approach to scheduling and supplier management with the goal of eliminating inventory. MRP is a planning tool — a software system that calculates ordering schedules from a production plan. In practice many companies use MRP to plan their ordering schedules while also applying JIT principles to minimise stock levels. They are complementary rather than mutually exclusive approaches. An exam question asking you to distinguish between them should focus on: JIT = production philosophy, pull-driven, minimal stock; MRP = computer-based calculation tool, push-driven from production schedule.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Lean Manufacturing and the Seven Wastes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="A systematic approach to manufacturing that focuses on eliminating all forms of waste — any activity that consumes resources without adding value for the customer.">Lean manufacturing</dfn> is a philosophy developed from the Toyota Production System that seeks to eliminate waste (called "muda" in Japanese) from every aspect of production. The central insight is that any activity consuming time, materials or labour without creating value for the customer is waste that should be eliminated or minimised. Seven categories of waste are identified in lean manufacturing, and understanding each one is essential for the OCR exam.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The seven wastes are: <strong>Transportation</strong> — unnecessary movement of materials between locations adds time and risk of damage without adding value. <strong>Inventory</strong> — holding more stock than needed ties up capital and space. <strong>Movement</strong> — unnecessary movement of people (operators walking to fetch tools, bending to reach components) wastes time and risks injury. <strong>Waiting</strong> — time spent by operators or machines idle (waiting for materials, instructions or the previous operation) adds lead time without adding value. <strong>Over-processing</strong> — carrying out more operations or finishing to a higher standard than the customer requires (for example, polishing a surface that will be hidden inside an assembly). <strong>Over-production</strong> — making more than is needed, earlier than needed — widely considered the most damaging waste because it generates all other wastes. <strong>Defects</strong> — products that do not meet specification require rework or scrapping, wasting all materials and labour invested in them.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Use the mnemonic TIM WOOD to recall the seven wastes: Transportation, Inventory, Movement, Waiting, Over-processing, Over-production, Defects. Practise explaining how reducing each one improves production.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven categories of lean waste are: Transportation, Inventory, Movement, Waiting, Over-processing, Over-production, Defects. The mnemonic TIM WOOD helps recall them. Over-production is considered the most damaging because it generates all other wastes — for example, producing too many parts creates excess inventory, which requires transportation, which risks damage (defects).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How does reducing each waste improve manufacturing performance?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Reducing transportation waste cuts lead time and damage risk. Reducing inventory frees up capital and floor space and improves responsiveness to design changes. Reducing movement waste by redesigning workstation layouts improves operator productivity and reduces musculoskeletal injury. Reducing waiting by synchronising production stages (balanced production flow) increases throughput. Reducing over-processing by specifying only the quality level customers require cuts cycle time. Reducing over-production by only making to order (pull production) eliminates the need to hold finished goods stock. Reducing defects cuts scrap, rework and warranty costs, and improves customer satisfaction. Each waste reduction directly improves efficiency, responsiveness and cost.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">Just-in-time manufacturing orders materials only when needed, cutting inventory costs but creating total dependency on supplier reliability — a late delivery stops production with no buffer.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Material requirements planning (MRP) is a computer-based system that calculates what to order, in what quantity and when, based on the production schedule and bill of materials — complementing JIT by providing the ordering schedule.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">The seven lean wastes — Transportation, Inventory, Movement, Waiting, Over-processing, Over-production, Defects (TIM WOOD) — represent all activities that consume resources without adding customer value; eliminating each directly improves throughput, cost and quality.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">When answering questions about the seven wastes, always identify the waste by its correct name and then explain specifically how eliminating it improves production — don't just say 'it saves time'. For example: 'reducing waiting waste by synchronising the output rate of each work station to match the next operation eliminates idle time and increases throughput without increasing machine investment.' For JIT questions, always include a limitation — production lines stopping due to a late delivery is the classic answer. If asked to compare JIT and MRP, use the contrast: JIT is a production philosophy; MRP is a computer-based calculation tool.</p>

