=== L10 Globalisation & the Future of Manufacture ===
id: db0d6e59-23b2-4bc3-9cb6-3d17cde07645
slug: lesson-10
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:53:32.630519+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 20

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How globalisation shapes manufacturing through international standards, employment, cost, sustainability and ethical.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Globalisation in Manufacturing?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="The process by which manufacturing, trade and business have become increasingly interconnected across national borders, with companies sourcing, producing and selling worldwide.">Globalisation</dfn> has fundamentally changed how products are made. A smartphone assembled in one country may contain a processor designed in the USA, memory chips from South Korea, a camera module from Japan, and a case moulded in China — all coordinated by a supply chain spanning dozens of countries. For engineering manufacturers, globalisation brings opportunities: access to lower-cost labour, specialist materials and larger markets. But it also introduces risks and responsibilities — around employment conditions, environmental impact, product quality standards and the ethics of international trade. Understanding globalisation's implications is an increasingly important part of the engineer's role.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">International Standards</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="Agreed technical specifications, such as ISO standards, that ensure products meet consistent quality, safety and interoperability requirements regardless of where they are manufactured.">International standards</dfn> are agreed specifications that allow manufacturers in different countries to produce components that are interchangeable, safe and of consistent quality. The most widely used system is the ISO (International Organisation for Standardisation) series. In engineering, standards govern dimensions (so an M10 bolt made in Germany will fit an M10 nut made in India), material properties, test methods, quality management systems (ISO 9001), and environmental management (ISO 14001). Without international standards, globalised manufacturing would be impossible — components from different countries would not fit together, and buyers could not rely on consistent quality from overseas suppliers.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">For individual manufacturers, achieving ISO certification signals to customers that processes meet a recognised benchmark, opening access to international markets. Meeting standards also drives internal quality improvements. However, achieving and maintaining certification requires ongoing investment in auditing, documentation and process improvement.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Remember two specific ISO standards for the exam: ISO 9001 (quality management systems) and ISO 14001 (environmental management systems). Also note that international standards allow components made in different countries to be interchangeable.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>International standards such as ISO 9001 (quality management) and ISO 14001 (environmental management) ensure that products made in different countries meet consistent quality and safety requirements. They enable interchangeable components, open access to global markets, and drive internal quality improvement — but require ongoing investment to achieve and maintain certification.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Influence on Employment Opportunities</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Globalisation has profound and often controversial effects on employment. When a manufacturer moves production to a country with lower labour costs, jobs are lost in the country of origin — a process known as offshoring. UK manufacturing employment fell sharply in the 1980s and 1990s as industries relocated to countries where wages were a fraction of UK rates. This can devastate communities dependent on a single factory or industry.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">However, globalisation also creates employment — in logistics, international trade, design, and the management of global supply chains. Higher-skill, higher-value roles may remain in the originating country while lower-skill production moves overseas. In the receiving country, factory jobs provide income and economic development, though the wages and conditions may be significantly below what would be acceptable in the manufacturer's home country. This raises ethical questions about whether companies have a responsibility to ensure fair wages and safe conditions throughout their supply chain.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Differences in employment conditions across countries</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">Employment conditions vary enormously between countries. Minimum wage legislation, maximum working hours, health and safety regulations, and the right to organise trade unions differ widely. A product manufactured in a country with weaker regulations may be cheaper partly because workers receive lower pay, work longer hours in less safe conditions, and have fewer rights than workers in the country where the product is sold. Consumers and companies face an ethical choice: prioritise lower product costs, or insist on supply chain standards that protect workers regardless of location. Initiatives such as ethical trade accreditation and supply chain auditing aim to address this imbalance.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Product Cost, Transportation and Sustainability</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Globalisation affects product cost in complex ways. Lower overseas labour costs reduce manufacturing cost, but the <strong>requirement for transportation</strong> — shipping, air freight, and road haulage across international supply chains — adds logistics cost, lead time and carbon emissions. Just-in-time supply chains that depend on rapid international freight are particularly vulnerable to disruptions such as port congestion, fuel price spikes or geopolitical events. The 2021 global shipping container shortage significantly increased manufacturing costs and delayed production across many industries.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The environmental, social and economic impact of transporting goods over long distances as part of a global supply chain, including carbon emissions, energy use and dependency on fossil fuels.">sustainability implications</dfn> of globalised manufacturing are significant. Long-distance freight generates substantial carbon emissions — particularly air freight, which emits around 50 times more CO₂ per tonne-kilometre than sea freight. This conflicts with manufacturers' sustainability targets and national carbon-reduction commitments. Reshoring (bringing production closer to the end market) and nearshoring (moving production to nearby countries rather than distant ones) are strategies companies use to reduce transportation emissions, shorten supply chains and improve resilience.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Learn the contrast: globalisation reduces manufacturing cost through lower wages but increases transportation cost and carbon footprint. Use 'reshoring' and 'nearshoring' as specific examples of companies responding to sustainability and supply chain resilience pressures.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Globalisation reduces manufacturing unit cost through lower overseas labour, but increases transportation cost, carbon emissions and supply chain risk. Air freight emits approximately 50 times more CO₂ per tonne-kilometre than sea freight. Reshoring and nearshoring are strategies to reduce transportation impact and improve supply chain resilience.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Economic, social, ethical and environmental considerations of globalisation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Economic: lower production costs benefit consumers through cheaper products and companies through higher margins, but risk deindustrialisation of high-wage economies and dependency on foreign suppliers for critical goods. Social: job losses in manufacturing communities can cause long-term unemployment and social decline; but global employment growth in developing countries has lifted millions out of poverty. Ethical: consumers in wealthy countries benefit from low prices partly achieved through low wages and poor conditions elsewhere — companies have a responsibility to audit their supply chains. Environmental: long supply chains increase carbon emissions; manufacturing in countries with weaker environmental regulation may involve practices (pollution, waste disposal) that would be illegal at home. These four dimensions always need to be considered together when evaluating any globalisation decision.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n17">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">International standards such as ISO 9001 and ISO 14001 enable globalised manufacture by ensuring consistent quality and interchangeable components, while opening access to international markets — but require ongoing investment to achieve and maintain.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Globalisation reduces manufacturing cost through lower overseas labour but increases transportation cost and carbon emissions, and raises ethical questions about employment conditions and supply chain responsibility.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Evaluating globalisation requires considering economic, social, ethical and environmental implications together — and companies must weigh cost advantages against supply chain resilience, sustainability targets and their responsibility to workers throughout the supply chain.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n16">For evaluate questions on globalisation, structure your answer across all four dimensions: economic (cost, employment, market access), social (job losses, community impact, development in receiving countries), ethical (supply chain conditions, fair wages), and environmental (transportation emissions, weaker regulations overseas). A top-band answer will not just list these — it will weigh them against each other and reach a substantiated judgement. For ISO standards questions, name specific examples: ISO 9001 for quality management, ISO 14001 for environmental management. When discussing transportation, always link it to both cost and carbon emissions — the exam expects both.</p>

