"Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that identifies the specific hazards (fine CRP dust — carcinogenic; noise above 85 dB(A); eye hazard from particles), assesses what PPE can offer (FFP3 respirator to filter fine particles; ear defenders; safety spectacles — these address the immediate personal exposure), then critically evaluates the limitations (PPE relies on consistent correct wearing and fit; it does not remove the dust from the environment so other workers nearby are also at risk; hearing protection only works if worn at all times in the noise zone; grinding CRP is a cumulative long-term health risk where any lapse in PPE has lasting consequences). Argues that engineering controls should be primary: local exhaust ventilation (LEV) at the grinding point removes particles before they become airborne; acoustic enclosures reduce noise at source; these protect all workers regardless of individual PPE compliance. Reaches a clear substantiated judgement — PPE is necessary but insufficient alone; LEV and enclosure are essential primary controls.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both sides evaluated with some application to CRP grinding context and an attempt at judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Relevant points but largely one-sided or lacking judgement.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points about PPE or hazards with little development. [8 marks]"
L10
"Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that constructs the argument for responsibility (consumers buy the final product believing the company endorses all aspects of its production; the company profits from lower costs partly achieved through poor conditions; reputational and legal risks — supply chain transparency legislation (e.g. UK Modern Slavery Act) means companies can be held accountable; the company has leverage to demand standards as a major customer). Then challenges this: the company cannot directly control independent suppliers in other countries; different legal frameworks mean what is legal abroad may be unacceptable at home; the cost of supply chain auditing is significant; forcing higher wages may make the supply uneconomic and shift production to less-auditable suppliers. A clear, substantiated judgement is reached — e.g. the company has both a moral and legal responsibility, but must act through specific enforceable supplier codes of conduct, regular auditing and willingness to exit relationships with non-compliant suppliers, rather than assuming responsibility can be delegated.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both sides evaluated with some application to the scenario and an attempt at judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Relevant points but largely one-sided.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points with little development or evaluation. [8 marks]"

