+    "expect": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that constructs the argument for responsibility (consumers buy the final product believing the company endorses all aspects of its production; the company profits from lower costs partly achieved through poor conditions; reputational and legal risks — supply chain transparency legislation (e.g. UK Modern Slavery Act) means companies can be held accountable; the company has leverage to demand standards as a major customer). Then challenges this: the company cannot directly control independent suppliers in other countries; different legal frameworks mean what is legal abroad may be unacceptable at home; the cost of supply chain auditing is significant; forcing higher wages may make the supply uneconomic and shift production to less-auditable suppliers. A clear, substantiated judgement is reached — e.g. the company has both a moral and legal responsibility, but must act through specific enforceable supplier codes of conduct, regular auditing and willingness to exit relationships with non-compliant suppliers, rather than assuming responsibility can be delegated.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both sides evaluated with some application to the scenario and an attempt at judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Relevant points but largely one-sided.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points with little development or evaluation. [8 marks]",
